<article xml:id="document" label="document">
  <title>2.1.11 and 2.1.15</title>
<section xml:id="sec-exterior-angle-theorem">
    <title>Exterior Angle Theorem 2.1.11</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-exterior-angles">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>
                    The measure of an exterior angle of a triangle
                    is greater than the measure of either of the
                    opposing angles of the triangle.
                </em>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <proof>
            <p>
      Suppose we have <m>\triangle ABC</m>. We can construct a ray from
      <m>\angle ACB</m> such that it goes through <m>\overline{AB}</m>
      and intersects it at point <m>E</m>, such that
      <m>A-E-B</m> and <m>E</m> is the midpoint of
      <m>\overline{AB}</m>. Thus,
      <m>\overline{AE} \cong \overline{EB}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      By the Ruler Postulate, we then construct point <m>F</m> on the ray
      such that <m>C-E-F</m>, with <m>E</m> as the midpoint of
      <m>\overline{CF}</m>. Therefore,
      <m>\overline{EF} \cong \overline{CE}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We do the same thing and construct a ray from
      <m>\angle CAB</m> that goes through <m>\overline{CB}</m>.
      Let this ray intersect <m>\overline{CB}</m> at point <m>G</m>,
      where <m>G</m> is the midpoint of <m>\overline{CB}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      By the Ruler Postulate, there is a point <m>H</m> on the ray such
      that <m>A-G-H</m> and
      <m>\overline{AG} \cong \overline{GH}</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We then construct a line <m>\overline{FH}</m> that goes through point
      <m>B</m>, such that <m>F-B-H</m> are collinear, with
      <m>\overline{CD}</m> and <m>\overline{FH}</m> intersecting at
      point <m>B</m>. Doing this, we have constructed
      <m>\triangle EFB</m> and <m>\triangle GBH</m>.
    </p>

    <image source="pasted-image-3.png"/>

    <p>
      By the Vertical Angle Theorem,
      <m>\angle FEB \cong \angle AEC</m>. Since
      <m>\overline{CE} \cong \overline{EF}</m> and
      <m>\overline{AE} \cong \overline{EB}</m>, it follows by SAS that
    </p>

    <me>
      \triangle AEC \cong \triangle FEB.
    </me>

    <p>
      Thus,
    </p>

    <me>
      \angle CAE \cong \angle EBF.
    </me>

    <p>
      By the Angle Addition Postulate,
    </p>

    <md>
      <mrow>
        m\angle ABD
        &amp;= m\angle EBF + m\angle FBD
      </mrow>

      <mrow>
        &amp;= m\angle CAE + m\angle FBD
      </mrow>

      <mrow>
        &amp;= m\angle CAB + m\angle FBD.
      </mrow>
    </md>

    <p>
      By Axiom 12,
      <m>m\angle FBD \ne 0</m>. Since
      <m>m\angle CAB</m> is being added to another angle,
    </p>

    <me>
      m\angle ABD > m\angle CAB.
    </me>

    <p>
      By the Vertical Angle Theorem,
      <m>\angle AGC \cong \angle HGB</m>. Since
      <m>\overline{CG} \cong \overline{GB}</m> and
      <m>\overline{AG} \cong \overline{GH}</m>, it follows by SAS that
    </p>

    <me>
      \triangle CAG \cong \triangle HBG.
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore,
    </p>

    <me>
      \angle GBH \cong \angle ACG.
    </me>

    <p>
      Also, by the Vertical Angle Theorem,
      <m>\angle GBH \cong \angle FBD</m>. Hence,
    </p>

    <me>
      \angle ACG \cong \angle FBD.
    </me>

    <p>
  Therefore, by the Angle Addition Postulate,
</p>

<md>
  <mrow>
    m\angle ABD
    &amp;= m\angle FBD + m\angle EBF
  </mrow>

  <mrow>
    &amp;= m\angle ACG + m\angle CAB.
  </mrow>
</md>

<p>
  Hence,
</p>

<md>
  <mrow>
    m\angle ABD &amp;> m\angle ACG
  </mrow>

  <mrow>
    m\angle ABD &amp;> m\angle CAB.
  </mrow>
</md>

  </proof>
</theorem>
</section>

<section xml:id="sec-exterior-angle-theorem">
    <title>Angle Angle Side 2.1.15</title>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-angle-angle-side">
    <statement>
      <p>
      <em>
       Two triangles are congruent if and only if two corresponding angles and a side not between them are congruent.
      </em>
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<proof>

      <p>
  Suppose we have two triangles, <m>\triangle ABC</m> and
  <m>\triangle DEF</m>, with
  <m>\angle BAC \cong \angle EDF</m>,
  <m>\angle ACB \cong \angle DFE</m>, and
  <m>\overline{AB} \cong \overline{DE}</m>.
      </p>

      <image source="pasted-image-4.png"/>

    <p>
  Assume, for contradiction, that
  <m>m\angle ABC > m\angle DEF</m>.
  We want to construct an angle <m>\alpha</m> such that
</p>

<me>
  \angle \alpha \cong \angle E.
</me>

<p>
  Using Axiom 18, we can construct a crossbar from
  <m>\angle B</m> that intersects <m>\overline{AC}</m>,
  constructing a point <m>G</m> such that <m>A-G-C</m>.
</p>

<p>
  We now also have
</p>

<me>
  \angle \alpha \cong \angle E.
</me>

<image source="pasted-image-6.png"/>

<p>
  By doing this, through angle-side-angle, the <m>\triangle ABG</m> that we constructed is congruent to <m>\triangle DEF</m> and we have <m>\angle AGB \cong \angle GCB \cong \angle DFE</m>. But <m>\angle AGB</m> is an exterior angle of <m>\angle BGC</m>, by the exterior angle theorem, <m>m\angle AGB \gt m\angle GCB</m> and <m>m\angle DFE</m> which contradicts the definition of congruent triangles.
</p>

    
        </proof>

</section>
</article>
</pretext>